  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Florida has received $23.4B in federal contract obligations so far in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 158,484 awards with an average award size of $148K. Obligations fell down 27.8% year over year, reflecting that current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date). The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $16B in the state this fiscal year. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing is the largest industry in Florida by obligations at $4.5B, followed by Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $2.3B and Offices of Physicians at $1.8B. Defense prime contractors dominate vendor totals, with LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION appearing twice among the top entries at $2.6B and $2.2B, and THALLE CONSTRUCTION CO INC at $1.9B. The figures below show fiscal-year comparisons and the top buying agencies, vendors, and industries that explain where the $23.4B is concentrated so far in FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What does FY2026 to date spending look like in Florida?",
        "body": "Florida received $23.4B in obligations in FY2026 to date, spread across 158,484 awards with an average award size of $148K. Obligations fell down 27.8% year over year, and FY26 totals are partial because the fiscal year is still in progress. Fiscal-year comparisons show a decline from FY25 at $32.5B and FY24 at $31.3B down to $23.4B in FY26."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries drive federal contracting in Florida?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $16B, making it the dominant buyer in the state. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $2.5B and the General Services Administration obligated $1.5B. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing accounts for $4.5B in obligations, Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction accounts for $2.3B, and Offices of Physicians account for $1.8B."
      }
    ]
  },
